ent-current-side arm now with a real deletion.\n\n  -> why it is ready: an observable, fail-closed admission rule, naming no file and only git concepts any reader can check.\n- [APPROVE] LLR-358 -> the obligation: once any spine carrier changes, read all four tiers at both trees from the first listed carrier, and refuse only a moved relationship. Prefix \"the index:\" or \"commit <sha10>:\". Refuse an unlistable difference or an unreadable current side, and count a bad or absent comparison side as moved. Admit the first commit, an untouched registry and an unmoved status. Apply the judgement at the staged check (current and merge parent) and at the lane walk (every parent), and invoke it from pre-commit as a commit step -> what its chain shows: it matches the Trust section's commit-judgement home and callers, and IF-308 names the one owner and its two requestors. TC-397's twenty-five cases hold each sentence, including both carrier orders and a lane merge against both parents -> why it is ready: a contract stated in observable inputs, outcomes and refusals. The pre-commit hook it names is a shipped boundary artifact, which the LLR tier may name.\n- [APPROVE] TC-397 -> the obligation: drive real repositories through the staged check and the lane walk over every edge, demotion, Founded, joint approval, no movement, no registry change, added and repaired registries, merge parents, first commits, unreadable sides and carrier order, and pin the rider, the step and the hook -> what its chain shows: every Expected clause matches an SR-246 or LLR-358 sentence and a named, existing case -> why it is ready: an observable Expected over existing cases, and Integration at Full is honest for git fixtures.\n\nOUTCOME: APPROVE rows=6\n\nSITTING: JUDGED kinds=first-approval\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3937,"cache_read_input_tokens":343507,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3937},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f031c6a5-ecf0-490f-9890-c7a8a87f92fc","uuid":"f95a3b17-aece-495a-b1c1-c1f13a5fde3c","timestamp":"2026-10-23T09:39:49.114Z","request_id":"req_011CgJwrhuvi7nw9WozSAN5r","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01QWnDxu8T2PQ6wdRqfkTqjb":{"file_path":"C:\\Projects\\ai-template.wt\\wi-850\\docs\\reviews\\wi-850\\006-ADJUDICATE-5559710.md","content":"# WI-850 checkpoint sitting at 5559710\n\n## first-approval\n\nSix lane-added rows await a first approval: SR-245 with LLR-357 and TC-396 (the standing finding), and SR-246 with LLR-358 and TC-397 (commit admission). I read them against:\n- SN-002 and SN-029;\n- the spec's Trust section and Done-when;\n- the roster's CONSISTENCY entry;\n- IF-308;\n- the second arms map, and the two tests added at 555971012 to close its last arms.\n\nChecks I ran at this tip:\n- `hats.py record docs/plans/WI-850/plan.perspectives.toml --check --strict` reports no findings. CONSISTENCY produced both SRs, which matches their `Hat-Refs`, and I judge every other applicable hat's no-finding adequate.\n- `trace.py --strict` exits 0. Its only advisories on these rows (no Form, unclassified) are ones nearly every SR in the registry carries.\n- The set's cases in `tests/test_parent_first.py`, `tests/test_parent_first_git.py` and `tests/test_trace_rules.py` pass: 50 run, 0 failed.\n\nBoth open arms of the map are closed:\n- `test_approval_is_read_in_any_letter_case` drives the shared predicate on a lower-case child and an upper-case parent.\n- `test_a_tier_absent_on_the_commit_side_supplies_no_rows` deletes a registry that held a Drafted parent and shows that the parent tree's rows are not read in its place.\n\nAll six rows are ready. Approving parents and children in one act is the shape SR-246 itself admits (a simultaneous parent-and-child approval passes), so the act this verdict authorizes meets the rule it approves.\n\n- [APPROVE] SR-245 -> the obligation: report every standing case where an Approved or Founded SR, LLR or TC cites a declared spine parent (SN, SR, or SR and LLR, respectively) that is neither. The report is one parent-first integrity finding that names the child and parent with both statuses, reads a missing status as no status, gives the approve-parent-or-return-child remedy, and fails strict integrity. Approval is recognized in any letter case, and an unknown word is unapproved. IF, B, DA, undeclared and -000 references are excluded. That finding is the one report for an Approved TC under a Drafted LLR -> what its chain shows:\n  - It is honestly DERIVED under SN-002 and SN-029, through CONSISTENCY. That hat's `listens_for` (\"related contracts or readers disagreeing ... one meaning given conflicting words\") names exactly the failure: an approved child asserting a complete chain its parent denies. The Rationale names the rejected alternative and feeds back to both needs.\n  - Every acceptance clause is a fit criterion of a Requirement clause.\n  - The finding text is pinned exactly. That is the honest level here, since the line crosses B-09 as the report.\n  - LLR-357 decomposes it, and TC-396 holds every arm, the letter-case arm now included.\n\n  -> why it is ready: one decision (the standing report), closed over a declared domain (the four spine tiers' declared rows), naming no file.\n  - Note, not a finding: the Requirement's long list attaches \"that lacks either status\" only after its last edge. The acceptance states the condition for all three edges, so the meaning is fixed there.\n  - Note, not a finding: \"replace the former non-failing advisory\" is history-voiced in a living cell. Its acceptance's standing form (\"No non-failing LLR-status advisory remains ...\") carries the obligation. A later amendment could restate both clauses in standing voice.\n- [APPROVE] LLR-357 -> the obligation:\n  - one approval predicate shared with the approval-act readers, case-insensitive for Approved and Founded, with an absent or unknown word unapproved;\n  - classification of only the declared SN-to-SR, SR-to-LLR and SR-or-LLR-to-TC edges;\n  - one finding per approved child under a non-approved declared parent, rendered with its exact text and \"no status\";\n  - none for an approved parent, a non-spine or undeclared reference, or a -000 row;\n  - the standing finding under the integrity label, which fails strict integrity and replaces the old advisory.\n\n  -> what its chain shows: it matches the Trust section's \"rule's one home\". TC-396's fifteen cases hold each clause, including the identity pin of the shared predicate. The case-sensitivity question the map raised, where the enum check still flags `approved` as outside the vocabulary while this rule reads it as approved, fails closed both ways. A lower-case child under a Drafted parent is found. A lower-case parent's cell carries its own integrity finding, so strict integrity still fails -> why it is ready: refactor-proof and pure, with no `shall`, and no own Hat-Refs, rightly.\n- [APPROVE] TC-396 -> the obligation: the shared case-insensitive predicate, each edge, Founded, missing and unknown statuses, the exact finding and remedy, the exclusions, strict integrity failing, and the advisory's absence -> what its chain shows: every Expected clause maps to an SR-245 or LLR-357 sentence and to a named, existing case -> why it is ready: an observable Expected over existing cases, and Full is honest for its trace-level cases.\n- [APPROVE] SR-246 -> the obligation: refuse a staged tree or lane commit that leaves an approved child under a non-approved declared parent when either status moved against any comparison parent. The refusal states the relationship, prefixed by the tree's identity. A staged tree is compared with its parent and the merge parent, and a lane commit with every parent. Unreadable differences and current-side registries refuse. An unreadable, unparseable or absent comparison side counts every status as moved. A joint approval, a first commit, a tree with no registry change, an absent current-side registry and a commit that moved neither status are left unrefused -> what its chain shows:\n  - It is DERIVED through CONSISTENCY on the same needs. Its Rationale argues why a standing report is too late at admission, and why one shared judgement for both routes was chosen.\n  - It is one decision (admission) separated from SR-245's standing report at the reconcile (D-009), so the two rows do not overlap.\n  - Every fail-open shape the Trust section rules is stated as a refusal or a \"counts as moved\".\n  - LLR-358 and TC-397 hold each arm, the absent-current-side arm now with a real deletion.\n\n  -> why it is ready: an observable, fail-closed admission rule, naming no file and only git concepts any reader can check.\n- [APPROVE] LLR-358 -> the obligation: once any spine carrier changes, read all four tiers at both trees from the first listed carrier, and refuse only a moved relationship. Prefix \"the index:\" or \"commit <sha10>:\". Refuse an unlistable difference or an unreadable current side, and count a bad or absent comparison side as moved. Admit the first commit, an untouched registry and an unmoved status. Apply the judgement at the staged check (current and merge parent) and at the lane walk (every parent), and invoke it from pre-commit as a commit step -> what its chain shows: it matches the Trust section's commit-judgement home and callers, and IF-308 names the one owner and its two requestors. TC-397's twenty-five cases hold each sentence, including both carrier orders and a lane merge against both parents -> why it is ready: a contract stated in observable inputs, outcomes and refusals. The pre-commit hook it names is a shipped boundary artifact, which the LLR tier may name.\n- [APPROVE] TC-397 -> the obligation: drive real repositories through the staged check and the lane walk over every edge, demotion, Founded, joint approval, no movement, no registry change, added and repaired registries, merge parents, first commits, unreadable sides and carrier order, and pin the rider, the step and the hook -> what its chain shows: every Expected clause matches an SR-246 or LLR-358 sentence and a named, existing case -> why it is ready: an observable Expected over existing cases, and Integration at Full is honest for git fixtures.\n\nOUTCOME: APPROVE rows=6\n\nSITTING: JUDGED kinds=first-approval\n"}}}
